/* ==============================================================
   03 — MovePose: naming a position in space
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   Cartesian targets, and how they differ from joint ones
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, no tool mounted
   WATCH     You name WHERE it ends up, not HOW it gets there
   TIME      ~20 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   FROM ANGLES TO SPACE

   MoveJoints asked for six angles. That is fine for the robot
   and useless for everyone else — nobody knows a part's location
   as a set of joint angles.

   MovePose asks for a POSE instead: a position and an
   orientation in ordinary three-dimensional space.

       MovePose(x, y, z, alpha, beta, gamma)

     x, y, z              where the tool tip is, in mm
     alpha, beta, gamma   how the tool is oriented, in degrees

   Read it as: "put the TRF at this pose, expressed in the WRF."

     TRF — Tool Reference Frame. The tool tip. By default it sits
           on the robot's flange; you move it to the real tip of
           the real gripper with SetTrf. See intermediate/02.
     WRF — World Reference Frame. The origin everything is
           measured from. By default it is the robot's base; you
           move it to a fixture or a table with SetWrf. See
           intermediate/01.

   Those two frames are what turn a robot into an application,
   and they are the reason this command is worth the extra
   concept. A pose stays correct when you change grippers or move
   the robot on the bench — you redefine a frame, and every pose
   in every program follows.

   THE ORIENTATION ANGLES

   alpha, beta, gamma are Euler angles, mobile XYZ convention.
   You will not compute these by hand and neither will a
   customer. In practice you jog the robot to the position you
   want and let the MecaPortal write the numbers for you: right
   click in the code editor, then "TO CURRENT POSITION", and it
   inserts the command with the current pose already filled in,
   using the current TRF and WRF.

   THE IMPORTANT SUBTLETY

   MovePose specifies the DESTINATION in Cartesian space. It does
   NOT specify the path. Internally the robot solves for the
   joint angles that produce your pose, then performs a
   joint-space move to them — exactly like MoveJoints.

   So the tool tip travels a smooth, repeatable arc, not a
   straight line. If you need the straight line, that is MoveLin,
   and it is the next file.

     MoveJoints  target as angles      path is an arc
     MovePose    target as a pose      path is an arc
     MoveLin     target as a pose      path is a straight line

   MovePose is the one you will use most: it takes coordinates a
   human can reason about, while keeping the speed and freedom of
   a joint-space move.

   ONE MORE THING

   A single pose can usually be reached by eight different arm
   postures — elbow up or down, wrist flipped or not. The robot
   picks one automatically. When you need to force the choice,
   that is SetConf, in intermediate/04.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(15)

// --- Start from the zero position -----------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)

/* This pose is the zero position expressed in Cartesian terms —
   the same robot posture as the line above, described the other
   way. Running it should produce no visible movement, which is
   the point: two vocabularies, one position. */
MovePose(190, 0, 308.9, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Now move in space ----------------------------------------
// Only Z changed: 60 mm lower. Compare this to MoveJoints, where
// "lower the tool by 60 mm" is not something you can express
// without doing the geometry yourself.
MovePose(190, 0, 248.9, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)

// Only Y changed: 80 mm across. Again, one number, one intent.
MovePose(190, 80, 248.9, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)

// Watch the tool tip on this one. We ask for a straight-line
// distance back across and up, and the tip visibly bows out on
// the way. That curve is the joint-space path. Nothing is wrong
// — we simply never asked for a straight line.
MovePose(190, -80, 308.9, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- Back to zero ---------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Jog the robot anywhere by hand, then right click in the
      editor and insert MovePose "TO CURRENT POSITION". Teaching
      a point takes about four seconds. This lands well with
      anyone who has taught points on a pendant.

   2. Run the last MovePose and trace the arc with your finger.
      Then open 04_move_lin.mxprog and run the equivalent line.
      The difference between the two is the whole lesson.

   NEXT   04_move_lin.mxprog — when the path itself matters.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
